From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] A navigation bar the reader put away answers only a swipe up from the bottom edge

The navigation bar is shown by default on this model, and the reader owns its visibility:
a tap on the bar's own key (patch 0077) puts it away, and a swipe up from the bottom edge
brings it back for good. Two states, no timers.

The state is the Settings.Global entry `readom_nav_locked` (1 = put away). It is a literal
name, not a new constant in `Settings.java`: a new constant would change framework.jar and
the ART boot image with it, and every application in /data would be recompiled at the next
upgrade. Hiding is still `policy_control = immersive.navigation=*`, written by the same
toggle, so the two settings agree and the state survives a restart.

Put away means put away. The first form of this patch guarded only the bar's *transient*
requests, and three reports came back from the device: a WeRead review dialog brought the
bar out, pulling the shade down brought it out, and a bar that was out could not be put
away again while either was on screen. Reading the policy end to end (32 paths, the audit
in `readom-port/reports/research/t32-nav-activation-audit.md`) found that every way the bar
becomes visible ends in `BarController.setBarShowingLw(true)`, and that only the transient
one of them was gated. So this patch gates the state in three layers, narrowest first:

  - the policy computation: while the bar is put away `updateSystemUiVisibilityLw()` forces
    `HIDE_NAVIGATION | IMMERSIVE_STICKY | LAYOUT_HIDE_NAVIGATION` into what the bars are
    asked to do, whatever the window on top asked for. That covers an application that
    shows a dialog and stops asking for immersive mode, the see-through-window rule of
    patch 0056 (which clears `HIDE_NAVIGATION` for a translucent application window so the
    reader keeps Back and Home -- WeRead's review dialog is exactly that window), and the
    input method's own clearing for the windows below it. The immersive flag is also what
    keeps the hidden-navigation input consumer off (`beginLayoutLw()`'s `navAllowedHidden`),
    so a touch anywhere cannot reach the "show the bar again" receiver, and what leaves the
    application laid out to the bottom edge -- no black band where the bar used to be;

  - the two places that show the window without consulting those flags: an expanded status
    bar (`finishPostLayoutPolicyLw()` -- the shade, the lock screen, a heads-up
    notification, all of which make the status bar window `MATCH_PARENT`) no longer shows
    the bar, and `layoutNavigationBar()` is no longer told that such a status bar may show
    it. Its own else branch exists for exactly that case ("unless we expanded the status
    bar"), and it was the one a shade pull-down took;

  - `BarController`: the window is shown and hidden in one place, `setBarShowingLw()`, so
    that is where the last word is. `setReadomLocked(true)` makes every `show` return
    without touching the window and makes `showTransient()` ask for nothing. This is the
    layer that also covers `adjustSystemUiVisibilityLw()`'s unhide flag and the pending
    show after a hide, which are reached from the window manager's own re-evaluation rather
    than from the policy's layout pass. Only the navigation bar's controller is ever set.

The reader's way back is `onSwipeFromBottom()`: while the bar is put away it writes 0 (and
drops `policy_control`), and the bar is simply shown -- no peek, no timer, nothing that
takes it away again. It is the only path left that can show the bar, so it does not ask
where the bar is: in landscape it is on the right, and the bottom edge is still where the
hand is. The strip it answers from is the bar's own height (`navigation_bar_height`, 90 px
on this panel) rather than the status bar's 45 px that the four edges used to share; the
gesture itself (45 px of travel, 500 ms) is unchanged.

One ordering detail: `updateSettings()` runs once from `init()`, before
`WindowManagerService` has a `WindowSurfacePlacer`, so the re-evaluation it asks for is
now guarded by `mSystemReady`. The state itself is still applied that early -- the first
layout pass reads it -- so a boot with the bar put away never has a frame that treats the
bar as unlocked.

A boot without this patch ignores `readom_nav_locked` and behaves as it always did.

Not run on a device yet when this patch was written; how to run it is in
`readom-port/reports/research/t34-nav-impl.md`.

